---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import ProteinIntakeCalculator from '@/components/tools/calc/ProteinIntakeCalculator.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="protein-intake-calculator">
  <ProteinIntakeCalculator client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How the protein intake calculator works</h2>
    <p>
      The protein intake calculator multiplies your body weight in kg by a number of grams per kg for your
      goal: <code>protein = weight × g/kg</code>. Most goals have a range, so the result is a low and a high
      figure, and the headline is the middle. Per meal is <code>protein ÷ meals</code>.
    </p>
    <p>
      The ranges per kg of body weight a day: sedentary adults 0.8 g (the RDA), adults over 65 1.0 to 1.2 g,
      regular exercise 1.2 to 1.6 g, building muscle 1.6 to 2.2 g, and losing fat while keeping muscle 1.8 to
      2.4 g. The RDA is set by the US National Academies. The range for older adults follows the PROT-AGE
      study group. Sports nutrition position stands give 1.2 to 2.0 g per kg for athletes.
    </p>
    <h2>Worked example</h2>
    <p>
      A person who weighs 75 kg and exercises regularly needs 1.2 × 75 = 90 g to 1.6 × 75 = 120 g of
      protein a day. The middle is 105 g, or 35 g in each of 3 meals. At the RDA the same person needs
      0.8 × 75 = 60 g, and to build muscle 120 to 165 g.
    </p>
    <h2>Limits</h2>
    <p>
      The ranges are for healthy adults. Pregnancy, breastfeeding and kidney or liver disease change the
      need, so ask a doctor or dietitian. The grams are per kg of total weight; with a high body fat
      percentage this overstates the need, so use your goal weight. The result is grams of protein, not
      grams of food.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
